    "question": {
      ":question/id": "978ff730-649b-506c-91ef-f0d4bc32310d",
      ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-easy/generated/a4456981161d52017e0a2ae669484fdc93d752317e1cac909ba9a5485a66aa66.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the vector $\\mathbf{a}$. Which of the following diagrams shows the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$?",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that $-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-easy/generated/c09bd21c0c9ed7ebfc230ae5e5af71e184376ed56f4ef1f761a88acf28773a5b.png)",
